00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:57.300
three but not in monitoring well two. Only four meters closer to the apparent source. The pattern looks

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.460
illogical until you remember you are standing on Wisconsin and Glacial Till and Outwash. Glacial

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:08.820
soils in Ontario, Alberta, and British Columbia are characterized by abrupt facies changes,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:15.460
sand lenses, silt drapes, cobblestringers, and diamicton over distances of centimeters to meters.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:23.660
These create preferential pathways that can transport dissolved BTEX or PFAS plumes tens of meters

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:30.600
away from the source while leaving adjacent soil or groundwater untouched. BC CSR, Protocol 4,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:37.600
and CCME Tier 2, risk assessment both require practitioners to characterize this heterogeneity rather

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.620
than assume uniform advection dispersion. The experienced practitioner walks the borehole logs

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:49.620
against the driller's notes for refusal, boulders, or sudden color change. Then they overlay slug test

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.420
hydraulic conductivity data and grain size curves before deciding on additional delineation.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:08:00.380
They also insist on continuous core or cone penetrometer testing where budgets allow because split

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:06.360
